  prose <<~PROSE
    A new mother, a person in a deep fever and a family in its first month of mourning share one difficulty: every crossing of the door stirs the house's resonance wake, and a newborn or a sick body feels it. The room behind is hung with damping felt. Outside, a neighbor takes a stool, lays a lap-board across the knees, loops the latch cord over one wrist and hangs a quiet-cloth over the knocker. For the month the door belongs to that neighbor. The household head stays inside and defers to the stool.

    The watcher follows an order posted by the midwife, the physic or the mourning elder. Trade callers and close kin on the list go in at their hour. Everyone else stops at the stool. They hand over broth in lidded pots, clean cloths, a borrowed cradle, a letter from off-hab. The watcher lifts every lid, tastes when the sender is unfamiliar, writes the message on the board, and carries in what the room can use. The caller gets a day to come back.
  PROSE
  prose <<~PROSE, section: :culture, heading: "A Month on the Step"
    Sitting a watch is dull work that fills with company. Neighbors bring the watcher a hot meal twice a day, usually better food than the household is eating. Children sit on the step to hear who was turned away. By the second week the stool has become the street's evening corner, and a good watcher spends as much effort sending friends home as refusing strangers, because laughter carries through felt.

    The office gives a neighbor power over another family's door, and streets watch how it is used. A watcher can keep out a mother-in-law the new parents cannot face, a creditor, or a lover the sick man's wife has never met. A watcher can also listen at the felt and come away knowing which brother was left out of a will. Households pick their watcher for discretion. They ask the woman three doors down who kept her mouth shut through the last mourning, and pass over the cousin who talks.

    Streets exchange the office as a debt. The household remembers who sat its month and owes a month back when that watcher's family needs one. Old debts surface at funerals. A widow may remind a family at the wake that her mother sat their grandfather's sickroom, and the family finds someone for the stool that week.
  PROSE
  prose <<~PROSE, section: :tensions, heading: "Crossing the Stool"
    A caller who walks past the stool shames the caller and leaves the household blameless. Neighbors stop greeting that person and children follow them down the street calling out the house number. Most people accept the refusal and sit to talk instead. The hard cases are the ones with a claim on the room: a son back from a long contract to find his father dying behind a stranger's stool, a husband's first wife arriving with the husband's other children. Watchers who admit such callers against the posted order take the blame if the patient worsens. Watchers who refuse them carry the grudge for years.
  PROSE
  prose <<~PROSE, section: :aftermath, heading: "Walking Out"
    On the last day the quiet-cloth comes down. The household walks out behind the midwife or the mourning elder into a street that has gathered at the door, and the new infant, the recovered patient or the widowed spouse is greeted by everyone who spent the month at the stool. The reopened table gives its first seat to the watcher. After a mourning month the first dish served is the one the watcher liked best from the pots carried in.
  PROSE

  cue "A caller slows as she reaches a door with felt hung over the knocker and a neighbor on a stool beside it, and hands up a lidded pot."
  cue "On the month's last day the felt comes down, a whole street crowds the step, and a neighbor in a work apron is led inside to the head of the table."
  affordance "A posted watch keeps traffic away from a damped room while the household still receives food, parcels, news and visitors on a day it can bear."
  pressure "A creditor pays a watcher to let him reach a dying debtor's bedside before the will is read, and the neighbor who saw the coins change hands has sat three watches for the family."
  pressure "An estranged son arrives on the ninth day of his father's sickroom month; the posted order leaves him off the list, and the watcher must choose whom to wrong."
  variation "Lying-in watches follow the midwife's order and admit her trade at any hour; mourning watches keep the burial custom's calendar; sickroom watches follow the physic's schedule and are the ones most often sat in shifts."
  variation "Dense districts keep the stool, cloth and lap-board; thin country marks the door and lets the nearest neighbor run the household's errands, and the debt is owed the same everywhere."
end
